            "sources": {
                "time": "A held action lasts the real time of that one piece of work over sixty: game time runs 30 times faster than real time, and boat work is halved again",
                "start": "A currach's gunwale was laid out on the ground first and her ribs set into it (Hornell 1938, the Boyne currach woven round its gunwale on the ground): two long laths alone in a pile lie at its two sides, whose ends at the bow the first rope draws together, spreading them to her beam; astern they run straight on until the second rope draws them together there",
                "endLashing": "Each end bound with some ten metres of cord, a quarter of an hour's work (an estimate)",
                "ribs": "Fitting one rib, about three minutes, as the canoe's: currach ribs were scalded, bent over the gunwale and set into it, built upside down (the Boyne and Aran currachs). Bent in wet, they hold their bend once dry, in a couple of days",
                "cover": "Stretching and sewing a small hide's worth of cover, some twenty minutes (an estimate); a bigger hide covers more at once and takes as much longer",
                "oars": "Her two oars shipped on their thole pins, the bull over the pin, a moment's work each: they stay aboard from then on, as on Aran, taken off only to dry",
                "lacing": "The hide laced to the gunwale at short intervals (Hornell 1938), every 5 cm, each turn round the gunwale and the hide's edge: the shape's builder counts the rope",
                "overturned": "Built upside down, her gunwale on the ground, she is turned over before she goes afloat (the Boyne currach was lifted out and turned upright to be covered; Aran currachs are carried upside down)",
                "framePart": "Her frame is the wood of her laths: its share and the weight it was set for are ash's, its strength and density are those of the wood split"
            },
            "overturnedPivotVoxels": [8, 5.458, 8],
